course: money
lesson: 4
title: Swaps and barter in the playground
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Barter is swapping things without using money, like two football cards for one Pokémon card in the playground.
explain: A swap is good when both people freely agree and each values what they get more than what they give, like Ella's old scooter for her neighbour's skateboard: win-win.
explain: Barter has a big problem: Aisha wants Jayden's slime, but Jayden wants crisps, not her stickers, so there is no deal; money fixes this because everybody takes money.
explain: A swap made with a threat is not a swap at all but bullying, and a "swap" where you give far more than you get is really a gift, so call it one if that is what you want.
fact: Barter means swapping goods or help without using money.
fact: Barter only works when each person has what the other wants; this is called a double coincidence of wants.
fact: A fair swap is one both people agree to freely; forcing someone with a threat is bullying, not trading.
fact: A good swap can leave both people happier, because each values the other's thing more; this is called win-win.
fact: Something is only worth what someone will actually pay for it; a price seen online is a guess until someone really buys it.
fact: If one person knows much more about what things are worth, they can get the better deal, so find out values before you swap.
fact: A gift is something you give for nothing back; a swap should be about equal value unless you mean it as a gift.
fact: You can swap time and help as well as things, like neighbours who babysit for each other.
fact: Many schools ban swapping food because some children have allergies, which can make them very ill.
fact: Schools sometimes ban card swapping when it leads to fights or tricks.
fact: When money stops working, for example after a war, people go back to barter, using things everyone wants like eggs or cigarettes.
fact: "Got done" is British slang for being tricked in a deal.
exercise: What is barter? | Swapping things without using money | Buying things with a bank card | Selling things for more than they cost | Saving money in a jar
exercise: Ollie swaps 2 cards for 1, and all three turn out to be common. How many cards is he down? | 1 | 2 | 3 | 0
exercise: Aisha has 10 stickers and no crisps. Jayden only wants crisps. Why is there no deal? | Jayden doesn't want what Aisha has | 10 stickers are worth too much for slime | Swapping stickers is against the law | Aisha must pay Jayden in gold
exercise: How does money fix Aisha's problem? | Everyone takes money, so she wouldn't need to find crisps | Money makes slime cheaper, so Jayden would give it away for less | Money makes Jayden suddenly want stickers instead of crisps | Money turns stickers into crisps
exercise: Kyle says "Give me 3 cards for 1 or you're dead." What is this? | Bullying, because a real swap must be agreed freely | A fair trade, because he did offer a card in return for them | A good deal for Ellie, because gold cards are very rare | Barter, which is always allowed
exercise: What did Ellie do right when Kyle threatened her? | Walked off and told a teacher | Gave him the cards to keep him calm | Threatened him back even harder | Offered him her lunch as well as the cards
exercise: Ella swaps her old scooter for a skateboard and both are happy. What is this called? | A win-win swap | A rip-off for one of them | A loan that must be paid back | A gift with nothing given back
exercise: Lena swapped a £3 gel pen for a 20p pencil. How much more value did she give than she got? | £2.80 | £3.20 | £2.20 | £1.80
exercise: Ibrahim swaps 2 biscuits a day for 1 bag of crisps, 5 days a week. How many biscuits does he give in a week? | 10 | 5 | 7 | 12
exercise: Why does Max's mum stop the food swaps? | Some children have allergies, so swapping food can be dangerous | Crisps are worth much more than biscuits | Food can only be bought, never swapped | Swapping food makes it go off faster
exercise: A website says a card is worth £10, but nobody at school will pay more than 50p. What is it worth at school right now? | 50p | £10 | £5.25 | £0
exercise: Freya does Sam's Tuesday duty, and Sam does her Friday. What is being swapped? | Time and help | Money for the dinner duty | Trading cards from home | Sweets from the tuck shop
exercise: Why might a school ban card swapping? | Swaps were causing fights and tricks | Cards are worth too much money | Teachers want the cards for themselves | Swapping is illegal in every country
exercise: After the war, why did people swap eggs and cigarettes? | Money had stopped working, so useful things became a kind of money | Eggs were the official money of Britain | Shops gave free eggs to everyone | People liked eggs more than money
exercise: How can you avoid "getting done" in a swap? | Find out what things are really worth first | Always say yes to the first offer | Only swap with older kids | Swap as fast as possible
test: Your mate goes "Pikachu for your shiny, deal or no deal, bruv?" You find out the shiny is way rarer. What's smart? | Say no thanks, or ask for a fairer swap | Swap anyway, all cards are worth the same | Swap and then tell everyone he's thick | Give him both cards to be nice
test: Matty grumbles, "bloody unfair, they banned swaps, it's MY cards!" What's the school's reason? | Swaps kept causing fights and tricks, so it's about safety | Teachers want to collect the cards and sell them online | The cards belong to the school once you bring them in | Swapping cards is a crime in Britain
test: Ollie reckons "if it's 2 for 1, I always win cos I get the rare one." Is he right? | Not always, the "rare" one might be common and he loses a card | Yes, getting the rare one always means you win | Yes, 1 card is always worth more than 2 | No, swaps can never be fair
test: Glasgow nan: "Nae swappin' food, son, wee Ruby's got a nut allergy." ("Nae" means no; "wee" means little.) Why? | Swapped food could make Ruby really ill | Nuts cost too much to give away | Ruby's mum wants the food back | Swapping food is only allowed on Fridays
test: Bus kid: "my card's worth a tenner online, no cap!" His mate offers 50p. What's it really worth to buy right now? | What someone will actually pay, here 50p | £10, because that's what the website says | £10.50, the two prices added | Nothing, cards are never worth money
test: What would happen if there were no money at all and you wanted bread? | You'd have to find a baker who wants something you have | You'd get bread free from any shop, because nobody could charge | You'd pay the baker with a promise to smile and say thank you | The bread would cost exactly one sticker, the same everywhere
test: Lena gave her £3 pen for a 20p pencil "cos Tia begged". Her mum says it's fine, if...? | ...she meant it as a gift, not a swap | ...Tia pays her back £3.20 by the end of the week | ...the pencil turns out to be made of real gold | ...she does the same swap again every single day
test: Grandad: "After the war we swapped a dozen eggs for a pair of socks." How many eggs is a dozen? | 12 | 10 | 6 | 20
test: Ella's neighbour says "win-win, innit" about the scooter swap. What does that mean? | Both of them ended up better off | Both of them lost the swap | Only the neighbour won the deal | Nobody got anything at all
test: Kyle says to Ellie "Gimme 3 cards or you're dead, lol jk." ("jk" means just joking.) What should she still do? | Say no, walk away and tell a teacher if he keeps on | Give him the cards, because he said it was only a joke | Threaten him back to make it even | Swap 2 cards so it's fairer
test: A Scouse lad goes, "I'll do your Friday dinner duty if you do me Tuesday, sound?" ("Sound" means okay.) What kind of swap is that? | A swap of time and help | A swap of money | A gift with nothing back | A threat dressed as a deal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice designated "barter"? | The exchange of goods or services without recourse to money | The purchase of goods by means of a bank card | The lending of money subject to repayment with interest | The accumulation of coins within a receptacle
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of barter as a means of trade. | Each party must possess precisely what the other desires | Barter is forbidden by law in all jurisdictions | Goods exchanged by barter invariably lose their value | Barter requires the involvement of a bank
exam: It is given that a pen valued at three pounds is exchanged for a pencil valued at twenty pence. Ascertain the excess of value surrendered by the owner of the pen. | Two pounds eighty pence | Three pounds twenty pence | Two pounds twenty pence | One pound eighty pence
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an exchange procured by means of a threat. | It constitutes bullying and not a genuine exchange | It constitutes a lawful and fair exchange | It constitutes barter of the most efficient kind | It constitutes a gift on the part of the threatener
